WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.542
Still images preserve discrete moments with potentially high detail.

00:00:03.542 --> 00:00:08.895
Video captures movement and sequence but creates more data and may reduce per-frame clarity.

00:00:08.895 --> 00:00:13.867
Choose the format that answers the objective rather than recording both by default.

00:00:13.867 --> 00:00:15.779
Altitude and angle shape perspective.

00:00:15.779 --> 00:00:19.603
Higher views cover more area but usually show less detail.

00:00:19.603 --> 00:00:22.662
Oblique views reveal building sides and terrain shape,

00:00:22.662 --> 00:00:24.956
while near-vertical views support plan-style comparison.

00:00:24.956 --> 00:00:28.016
Angle also changes scale, occlusion, and apparent distance.

00:00:28.016 --> 00:00:32.605
Field of view is the area visible through the camera or sensor.

00:00:32.605 --> 00:00:35.664
Lens choice, altitude, orientation, and cropping affect coverage.

00:00:35.664 --> 00:00:40.253
Record enough context to locate the subject without collecting a wide area

00:00:40.253 --> 00:00:42.547
that the mission does not need.

00:00:42.547 --> 00:00:47.136
Overlap helps mapping and before-and-after comparison by ensuring adjacent views share features.

00:00:47.136 --> 00:00:50.578
Consistent orientation, route, altitude, and capture interval improve repeatability.

00:00:50.578 --> 00:00:54.019
However, repeated collection should remain within the authorized purpose.

00:00:54.019 --> 00:00:55.167
Stable capture matters.

00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:57.843
Sudden motion, vibration, poor focus, haze, glare,

00:00:57.843 --> 00:01:00.903
and aggressive camera movement can make footage unusable.

00:01:00.903 --> 00:01:06.639
Pause or repeat a safe capture rather than rushing through a checklist while conditions deteriorate.

00:01:06.639 --> 00:01:11.610
Review a sample for exposure, focus, framing, storage, and metadata when operationally safe.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:13.904
Clear imagery does not remove uncertainty,

00:01:13.904 --> 00:01:17.729
but poor imagery can create false detail and unreliable interpretation.

00:01:17.729 --> 00:01:19.641
Quality begins with deliberate collection.

00:01:19.641 --> 00:01:23.082
Collection teams should prefer a small set of well-documented,

00:01:23.082 --> 00:01:26.524
usable images over a large volume of rushed footage.

00:01:26.524 --> 00:01:32.260
Excess data increases review effort and can obscure the evidence that actually answers the objective.
